# Project: pytest Python Test Automation Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use pytest as the primary test runner for all Python test types (unit, integration, e2e)
- Organise tests in a dedicated `tests/` directory mirroring the `src/` package layout
- Place shared fixtures and plugins in `conftest.py` files at the appropriate scope level
- Separate unit, integration, and end-to-end tests into distinct subdirectories
- Use `pyproject.toml` (or `pytest.ini`) as the single source of truth for pytest configuration

```
project/
├── src/
│   └── mypackage/
│       ├── __init__.py
│       └── services/
│           └── user_service.py
├── tests/
│   ├── conftest.py          # Project-wide fixtures and plugins
│   ├── unit/
│   │   ├── conftest.py      # Unit-scope fixtures
│   │   └── services/
│   │       └── test_user_service.py
│   ├── integration/
│   │   ├── conftest.py
│   │   └── test_user_api.py
│   └── e2e/
│       └── test_user_flows.py
├── pyproject.toml
└── pytest.ini               # or [tool.pytest.ini_options] in pyproject.toml
```

## Coding Standards
- Follow PEP 8; enforce with `ruff` or `flake8` and format with `black`
- Add type hints to all function signatures, including test functions and fixtures
- Maximum line length: 88 characters (Black default)
- Name test files with `test_` prefix; name test functions with `test_` prefix
- Use descriptive test names that read as plain-English requirement statements

## Test Organisation
- Group related tests in classes only when they share meaningful state or fixtures; prefer module-level functions otherwise
- Use `pytest.mark` to tag tests by type (`@pytest.mark.unit`, `@pytest.mark.integration`) and register custom marks in `pyproject.toml`
- Use `pytest.mark.parametrize` to cover multiple input cases without duplicating test bodies
- Keep each test focused on a single behaviour: one logical assertion per test

## Best Practices
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern with a blank line separating each phase
- Prefer fixtures over `setUp`/`tearDown`; use `yield` fixtures for teardown logic
- Never share mutable state between tests; each test must be fully independent
- Avoid `time.sleep()` in tests; use retries or event-driven waits instead
- Use `pytest-xdist` (`-n auto`) for parallel execution of independent test suites

## Fixtures
- Define fixtures at the narrowest scope needed (`function` → `class` → `module` → `session`)
- Use `yield` for fixtures that require cleanup; code after `yield` runs as teardown
- Place shared fixtures in `conftest.py`; keep test-file-only fixtures in the test file itself
- Return only what the test needs; avoid "god fixtures" that return entire application state

```python
# conftest.py
import pytest
from mypackage.db import Database

@pytest.fixture(scope="session")
def db_engine():
    engine = Database.connect("sqlite:///:memory:")
    yield engine
    engine.dispose()

@pytest.fixture
def db_session(db_engine):
    session = db_engine.new_session()
    yield session
    session.rollback()
    session.close()

@pytest.fixture
def user_factory(db_session):
    def _make(name: str = "Alice", email: str = "alice@example.com"):
        from mypackage.models import User
        user = User(name=name, email=email)
        db_session.add(user)
        db_session.flush()
        return user
    return _make
```

## Parametrisation
- Use `pytest.mark.parametrize` to express input/output tables; keep parameter tuples small and readable
- Provide `ids` for parametrised cases to produce readable test node names
- Combine multiple parametrize decorators for combinatorial coverage only when the matrix is intentional

```python
import pytest
from mypackage.validators import validate_email

@pytest.mark.parametrize(
    ("email", "expected"),
    [
        ("user@example.com", True),
        ("bad-email",        False),
        ("",                 False),
        ("a@b.c",           True),
    ],
    ids=["valid", "no-at", "empty", "short-domain"],
)
def test_validate_email(email: str, expected: bool) -> None:
    assert validate_email(email) == expected
```

## Mocking
- Use `pytest-mock`'s `mocker` fixture instead of `unittest.mock.patch` decorators for cleaner scoping
- Prefer `mocker.patch.object(obj, "method")` over patching by string path when possible
- Use `mocker.MagicMock` with `spec=` set to the real class to catch interface mismatches early
- Assert on mock calls with `assert_called_once_with` / `assert_any_call`; avoid `assert mock.called` alone
- Restore all patches automatically — `pytest-mock` handles this; never call `patcher.stop()` manually

```python
def test_send_welcome_email(mocker):
    mock_send = mocker.patch("mypackage.email.send_email")
    from mypackage.services import UserService

    UserService.register(email="new@example.com")

    mock_send.assert_called_once_with(
        to="new@example.com",
        subject="Welcome!",
    )
```

## Markers and Skipping
- Register all custom markers in `pyproject.toml` under `[tool.pytest.ini_options] markers`
- Use `@pytest.mark.skip(reason=...)` and `@pytest.mark.skipif(condition, reason=...)` with a meaningful reason
- Use `@pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True, reason=...)` for known bugs tracked in your issue tracker
- Use `@pytest.mark.slow` to tag long-running tests and exclude them from the default run (`-m "not slow"`)

```toml
# pyproject.toml
[tool.pytest.ini_options]
markers = [
  "unit: fast, isolated unit tests",
  "integration: tests requiring external services",
  "slow: tests taking more than 5 seconds",
  "smoke: critical path sanity checks",
]
```

## Test Data Management
- Use factory fixtures (or `factory_boy`) for reusable, composable test objects
- Store static test data in `tests/fixtures/` as JSON or YAML files; load via a fixture
- Generate dynamic or random data with `faker` to prevent false positives from hardcoded values
- Never commit secrets or real credentials to test data; use environment variables or `pytest-dotenv`

## Assertions and Error Handling
- Use plain `assert` statements — pytest rewrites them for detailed failure output
- Use `pytest.raises(ExceptionType)` as a context manager to assert on expected exceptions
- Inspect `exc_info.value` for exception message content rather than relying on string matching alone
- Use `pytest.approx()` for floating-point comparisons

```python
def test_divide_by_zero() -> None:
    from mypackage.math import divide

    with pytest.raises(ZeroDivisionError, match="division by zero"):
        divide(1, 0)

def test_float_sum() -> None:
    assert 0.1 + 0.2 == pytest.approx(0.3)
```

## Coverage
- Configure `pytest-cov` in `pyproject.toml`; run with `--cov=src --cov-report=term-missing`
- Set branch coverage (`--cov-branch`) to catch untested conditional paths
- Define minimum thresholds with `--cov-fail-under=80` (raise as the suite matures)
- Exclude generated code, migrations, and `__main__` blocks from coverage with `# pragma: no cover`

```toml
[tool.coverage.run]
source = ["src"]
branch = true
omit = ["*/migrations/*", "*/__main__.py"]

[tool.coverage.report]
fail_under = 80
show_missing = true
```

## Plugins and Tooling
- `pytest-mock` — preferred mocking interface
- `pytest-cov` — coverage integration
- `pytest-xdist` — parallel test execution
- `pytest-asyncio` — `async def` test support (set `asyncio_mode = "auto"` in config)
- `pytest-httpx` / `responses` — HTTP client mocking
- `factory_boy` — model factory fixtures
- `faker` — realistic fake data generation
- `pytest-dotenv` — `.env` loading for integration tests

## CI/CD Integration
- Run the full suite with `pytest -p no:randomly` in CI to detect order dependencies
- Emit JUnit XML with `--junitxml=reports/junit.xml` for pipeline test result parsing
- Cache the `.pytest_cache` directory between CI runs to speed up collection
- Split test runs by marker in parallel CI jobs: `pytest -m unit`, `pytest -m integration`
- Fail the pipeline immediately on the first collection error (`--co -q` in a pre-check step)

## Reporting
- Use `pytest-html` for local HTML reports: `--html=reports/report.html --self-contained-html`
- Use `pytest-allure` for rich Allure reports with steps, attachments, and history
- Always capture stdout/stderr on failure with `-s` locally; suppress it in CI with the default capture
- Use `pytest --tb=short` in CI for concise tracebacks; use `--tb=long` when debugging locally

## Documentation
- Document fixture purpose and expected teardown behaviour in a short docstring
- Maintain a `tests/README.md` describing how to run each test suite and required environment variables
- Add a `CONTRIBUTING.md` section on writing tests, naming conventions, and marker usage

Remember to leverage pytest's powerful fixture system and plugin ecosystem to keep tests fast, isolated, and expressive — favour composition over inheritance, and let `conftest.py` files define the natural scoping hierarchy of your test infrastructure.
